Cell["\<\
Entries answering to none of them are simply expressions, and make a \
perfectly good array:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0419b164-e06a-a14e-84b7-3302780c850a"],

Cell["\<\
{ArrayDomain @ ArrayObject[{\"a\", \"b\"}], ArrayDomain @ ArrayObject[{x, \
y}],
  ArrayDomain @ ArrayObject[{1, \"a\"}]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"eda531f4-cc28-9a43-9bd8-a8497b0dc248"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"Expression\"\>", ",", "\<\"Expression\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"Expression\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"4847146a-\
5e59-f145-9f3f-efc57a6aff6a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "A computed domain rather than a declared one is what lets the entries be \
anything. There is nothing to verify and nothing to reject, so an array of \
labels or of half-built expressions needs no special case: it is an array \
whose domain happens to be \"Expression\". \"Boolean\" means the entries are \
True and False; entries 0 and 1 are integers, and the two-element field is \
FiniteField[2, 1] \[LongDash] three different things that all get called \
boolean in casual use, kept apart here."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"dcaf970b-bd71-6745-a1a4-33e0c10e7f81"],

Cell["2. Generated entries", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"8fb8fedd-e258-3d43-b934-cdc07d22d778"],

Cell["\<\
Entries do not have to be given. GenerateSymbolicArray builds them from a \
name and the indices, and gives back an array like any other:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f524cea0-cb35-6a4e-916a-b15797fc7458"],

Cell["GenerateSymbolicArray[a, {2, 3}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6d7a763e-327a-ca47-82b1-4dcc23ac523e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{
   TagBox["ArrayObject",
    "SummaryHead"], "[", 
